## Conversation Summary

Date: 2026-09-17

### Objective

Design an object-oriented architecture and class model for LibConnect.

### Key decisions

- Separate `Book` as title metadata from `BookCopy` as a physical borrowable item.
- Model users, catalogue items, loans, reservations, fines, and notifications as separate domain concepts.
- Keep controllers thin, place business rules in services, and isolate persistence behind repositories.
- Use shared authentication and role-specific controllers for Librarian and Member workflows.
- Keep domain models independent of JavaFX and file/database details.

### Work completed

- Produced a high-level architecture with authentication, role controllers, catalogue, loan, reservation, fine, and notification services.
- Defined the main classes, relationships, service responsibilities, and useful status enumerations.
- Identified the core end-to-end workflows for both roles: member borrowing/renewal/return and librarian management/monitoring/fine/alert operations.

### Open items / blockers

- The diagram was a design baseline; implementation contracts and exact persistence formats still required agreement.

### Next steps

- Convert the conceptual model into stable interfaces, validation rules, and testable service boundaries.
